{"episodes":{"210509":"On March 26, 2026, a potent cold front tracked across central Indiana, interacting with a warm and unstable airmass to trigger severe storms.  Severe thunderstorms developed rapidly, producing large hail and damaging wind gusts across northern central Indiana that downed trees and power lines, leading to over 1,700 power outages as residents dealt with the abrupt onset of hazardous weather.||Significant structural damage was observed, including a destroyed pole barn and a garage roof failure in Clinton County. While the environment supported supercellular rotation and prompted numerous tornado warnings, the only severe modes were straight-line damaging winds and large hail, with reports of 1.5-inch hail in Veedersburg. The convective activity gradually weakened as it progressed south of the I-70 corridor and encountered a more stable airmass, effectively concluding the active weather event for the region.","210769":"In southeast Ohio, showers primarily grazed locations to the north of US-50 in the pre-dawn hours of March 27th. A cold front brought a band of heavier precipitation through the entirety of southeast Ohio and into the tri-state area by mid-morning. Thereafter, a few additional rounds of light precipitation affected areas south of US-50 prior to the departure of precipitation during the afternoon.||All said, around a half inch to just over an inch of rain fell over southeast Ohio, leading to minor flooding along portions of Duck Creek, the Little Muskingum, and Sunday Creek. Impacts were primarily relegated to flooding of adjacent low-lying areas, as well as a few roads.","210771":"Scattered thunderstorms moved across northeast Illinois during the afternoon and evening of March 26th producing isolated large hail.","211146":"At the surface, a cold front moved from the southern Great Lakes region to the Upper and Middle Ohio Valley during the early afternoon through late evening hours of the 26th as a low moved east-northeastward from southern Quebec to the Gulf of Saint Lawrence. Along and ahead of the front, scattered multicell and isolated supercell thunderstorms impacted northern Ohio as the storms persisted generally eastward or southeastward during the late afternoon through mid-evening hours of the 26th, amidst moderate to strong effective bulk shear and weak to moderate MUCAPE. The storms produced sporadic straight-line convective wind damage and severe hail. Two weak tornadoes occurred; one in Morrow County and the other in Knox County, where mixed layer LCL's were favorably-low and surface-based ESRH was maximized due to southwesterly low-level jet development.","211222":"A cold front moved through the Great Lakes region, producing strong thunderstorms. Some storms produced damaging wind.","211225":"A slow moving cold front moved through the Great Lakes region on the 26th and 27th, accompanied by showers and thunderstorms. Some storms produced heavy rainfall, resulting in instances of flooding and high water.","211589":"Thunderstorms developed a long a cold front that was sagging southward across the area during the afternoon and evening hours. One of these storms became severe producing hail up the size of quarters during the evening in Van Buren County.","211598":"Thunderstorms developed a long a cold front that was sagging southward from Iowa into far northeast Missouri during the afternoon and evening hours. These storms remained below severe limits produced hail up to the size of pennies.","211741":"A southeastward moving cold front sparked thunderstorms in the afternoon and evening. Good moisture return for March pushed dew points above 60 degrees with air temperatures in the mid 70s. A 1000-1500 J/kg MUCAPE gradient nosing in from the west with potent EBWD values of 50-60 knots supported severe thunderstorms with large hail up to golf ball size, sporadic wind damage, and urban flooding with training storms.","211758":"A southeastward moving cold front sparked thunderstorms in the afternoon and evening. Good moisture return for March pushed dew points above 60 degrees with air temperatures in the mid 70s. A 1000-1500 J/kg MUCAPE gradient nosing in from the west with potent EBWD values of 50-60 knots supported severe thunderstorms with large hail up to golf ball size, sporadic wind damage, and urban flooding with training storms.","211849":"An unseasonably warm and humid airmass developed across central Illinois on March 26, 2026, with some locations recording record high temperatures in the upper 80s and lower 90s. Dewpoints also surged into the lower 60s. As a cold front approached during the evening, scattered to widespread strong to severe storms developed along and ahead of the front, primarily in areas north of I-70. These storms produced hail as large as golf balls, as well as a few wind damage reports.","212349":"A moist and unstable weather pattern affected Puerto Rico from March 24 through March 28, 2026, as a mid- to upper-level trough interacted with abundant tropical moisture and favorable upper-level dynamics. Periods of heavy showers and thunderstorms developed across the island, resulting in flooding concerns, urban and small-stream flooding, and rapid rises on rivers and streams. The threat was enhanced by strong upper-level support associated with a strengthening jet stream and persistent moisture across the region."},"events":[{"begin":"2026-03-27T00:00Z","county":"Hardin","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"4.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2026-03-27T00:02Z","endLat":40.73,"endLon":-83.83,"episode":211222,"id":1319786,"injuries":0,"lat":40.73,"location":"2.32 NNE Alger","lon":-83.83,"magnitude":50.0,"magnitudeType":"EG","narrative":"A home sustained roof and siding damage. Additionally, several large tree branches were snapped.","source":"Emergency Manager","state":"OH","type":"Thunderstorm Wind","wfo":"ILN"},{"begin":"2026-03-27T00:00Z","county":"Seneca","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2026-0